## 2. Matcher, `zrmd/vector/fontfit.py` (12 October 2026)

Given a reference text crop and its known string (`crop_to_mask` turns a gray crop into a coverage mask), `match_font(ref, text)`:

1. coarse, every usable file (661): size from the ink height, wght from the measured stem through the file's stem curve, tracking from the ink width, origin from the ink box; one score per file;
2. fine, the top_k files: Nelder-Mead on size, sub-pixel origin, tracking and every fitted axis present (wght, wdth, opsz, slnt) on blurred masks, then decoupled rounds (size and origin y on the row profile, one x shift per glyph folded into tracking and per-glyph deltas, axes by bounded 1-D searches);
3. polish (`vector.fit.polish`): profiled axis polish of the best fit, each axis location gets its own placement refit. This is what moved Source Serif 4 from IoU 0.9756 (wght error 11.8) to 0.9983 (wght error 0.09).

Match rule (12 October 2026, `vector.fit.match`): IoU at least 0.99 and mean edge distance at most 0.3 px.

## 3. Parametric font creator, `zrmd/vector/fontcreate.py` (12 October 2026)

When no free family reaches the match rule, the creator starts from the matcher families (multi-start, `vector.create.starts`, TrueType outlines only), instances the base at its axes with fontTools `varLib.instancer`, and fits parameters so the rendered string lands on the reference mask, then builds a real `.ttf` with fontTools. The font is a pure function of (base file, `params.json`), schema `zrmd/font-params@1`:

| Parameter | Effect |
|---|---|
| `base.axes` | location of the free base font (searched on a grid, refined) |
| `global.weight_units` | every point moves along its ink-growing normal |
| `global.contrast_units` | extra normal offset times (abs nx minus abs ny): stems thicken, horizontals thin |
| `global.x_height_scale` | lowercase scale between baseline and x-height, blending back to the ascender |
| `global.overshoot_units` | round parts beyond baseline, x-height and cap height grow |
| `glyphs.<name>.scale_x`, `advance_units` | per-glyph width and spacing |
| `glyphs.<name>.offsets` | per-point residual offsets in font units, smoothed along the contour |
| `layout` | size, origin, tracking of the line |

Fitting stages (12 October 2026): axis grid search; layout rounds (spacing by least squares on per-name advances, then globals with line size and origin); residual point offsets by edge snapping (area difference on a strip across each edge, damped, smoothed, kept only when the loss improves).

### Fix of this session (12 October 2026)

The previous run was RED on one case: the creator rebuilt the target at IoU 0.9703 (start Montserrat, wght 557.9 for a truth of 480, weight_units minus 2.2 for a truth of plus 9). Diagnosis by measurement, not by guess:

| Probe at the true wght 480 | Result |
|---|---|
| truth params through `render_params` | IoU 0.9948, so the model can represent the target |
| default creator layout | IoU 0.949, size stuck at 91.84 for a truth of 90, x_height_scale 1.027 for 1.05 |
| blur schedule alone (sigma 6, 3, 1) | 0.948, still the same basin |
| size unit rescaled to the cap height | 0.949, same basin |
| size factor 0.99 from the matcher size | 0.858 (line drift breaks spacing) |
| five size starts with tracking compensation, sigma 6, 3, 1 | **0.9935**, size 90.04, x_height_scale 1.047, weight 8.88 |

Cause: the matcher size belongs to an unmodified font, so a taller x-height is first explained by a bigger line size, and the local least squares stay in that basin; a plain size change then makes the line drift by several pixels, beyond what a local fit can see. Fix, added in `fontcreate._fit_at` and driven by settings (HDA law):

- `vector.create.size_starts` = [0.97, 0.98, 0.99, 1.0, 1.01]: the first layout round runs from each line size factor, with tracking compensated so the line length is kept, and the best loss is kept;
- `vector.create.layout_sigmas` = [6, 3, 1]: coarse-to-fine blur per layout round;
- `vector.create.size_unit` and `vector.create.global_x_scale` are optional switches, unset by default (measured neutral or divergent: `x_scale="jac"` overflowed in scipy and produced an empty path).

Landscape after the fix: at wght 480 the layout reaches 0.9935, at wght 557 only 0.967, so the axis search now finds the true location.

